  "description": "The ultimate steel single speed cross bike. The Nature Boy you know and love with a high end Columbus Zona tubeset, Press Fit 30 bottom bracket, and lighter non removable brake posts.\n\nIt's about a half pound lighter than the standard Nature Boy, the Zona tubes give it a silky smooth ride, and the PF30 BB provides an excellent sprinting platform.\n\nIt pops up once per year, and this time they will be available in June as framesets and for the first time completes, with a lovely red / white / blue fade paint scheme. This year's batch is limited to 65 framesets and 40 complete bikes.\n\nThe complete feature a Cane Creek 40 headset, Velocity A23 Rims, lightweight Formula cassette hubs, a Surly Cog and spacer kit (to allow you to run two cogs if you wish), and an FSA Gossamer Press Fit 30 crankset. Ready to race.\n\nFrame: Columbus Zona tubeset. Double butted down, top, and seat tubes. Externally tapered, ovalized, and dimpled chain stays, tapered seat stays.\n\nFork: 4130 ChroMoly double butted tapered fork blades, lugged crown and matching fork end. Designed to be interchangeable with most aftermarket\n\nInternal cable routing: The internal rear brake routing not only improves the beauty of the frame, but also provides the ultimate in comfort when grabbing the top t\n\nSignature Dropouts: Investment cast from stainless steel, featuring built in chain tensioners, and the timeless Hennepin Bridge icon.\n\nSubtle fender mounts: We hope you ride this bike in all conditions all year long, so with that in mind we added fender mounts. Since this is a race bike and we t\n\nE.D. Finish: The entire bike is E.D. coated inside and out. E.D. coating not only improves the quality and durability of the paint, but is the best rust\n\nCross race geometry: This bike was designed to go real fast off road. It features aggressive yet traditional cyclocross racing geometry and goes like hell.\n\nTire clearance: You can fit 38’cs in this bad boy front and rear, no problem. If you’re running fenders it’ll clear 35’s.\n\nSteel tubing: Steel remains the preferred material for those who appreciate ride quality. It is the benchmark that all other frame materials are compared",
